@name Diamond triplet 50mm f/2, diamond <= 20 mm
# Diamond triplet with a 20 mm diamond, 50 mm f/2: aspheric LuAG front
# element, N-LASF46B negative, stop, diamond rear element (clear diameter
# limited to 20 mm, the size single-crystal CVD diamond plates come in).
# Why: the realistic diamond. The diamond sits right behind the stop, where
# the beam is narrowest.
# Bar: 50 mm, 36 x 24, f/2, <= 3 elements, white-light RMS <= ~8 um at 0, 0.7, 1.0
# field, distortion <= 1 %, corner illumination >= 0.35, back focus >= 20 mm.
# Design: lens-render optimize (global search, equal budget for every material
# set): spherical screening of all glass combinations of each topology, then
# aspheric refinement of the best combinations, then 24 starts with one basin
# hop each (this job). Merit: RMS spot at 0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 1.0 field,
# 440/486/546/656 nm (0.5:1:2:1), object at infinity (1000 m); glass aspheres
# departure <= 0.5 mm. Experiment: lenses/novel/README.md.
# Numbers (lens-render numbers, f/2, infinity): RMS 10.8 / 15.1 / 17.6 um at
# 0 / 0.7 / 1.0 field (flat 440-660 nm: 11.7 / 14.6 / 19.1), distortion -0.73 %,
# corner illumination 0.35, length 72.6 mm, back focus 39.5 mm; diamond
# 19.7 mm across, 5.8 mm thick. At 2.5 m: 12.0 / 16.4 / 14.8 um. Worse than the
# glass triplet.
#
# Generated by `lens-render optimize lenses/novel/diamond-triplet-20mm-f2.toml`.
# RMS spot (4 wavelengths) at f/2, object at 1000 m, by image height
# (fraction of 21.63 mm): 0: 11.4, 0.3: 8.5, 0.5: 11.5, 0.7: 13.9, 0.85: 12.4, 1: 18.1 µm. Units: mm.
#
# Global search: place 1, merit 1.0081e3 (start 18, seed 1).
#
